⟦P0⟧Go Security Policy⟦P1⟧Overview⟦P2⟧This document explains the Go Security team's process for handling issues⟦P3⟧reported and what to expect in return.⟦P4⟧Reporting a Security Bug⟦P5⟧All security bugs in the Go distribution should be reported by email to⟦P6⟧⟦P7⟧⟦P8⟧⟦PB1_9⟧. This mail is delivered to⟦P10⟧the Go Security team.⟦P11⟧Please format the subject of your email as "Vulnerability: {package name}:⟦P12⟧{one-line summary}", and avoid sending attachments in your email unless strictly⟦P13⟧necessary in order to avoid your message being marked as spam.⟦P14⟧If desired, please include your preferred attribution as part of the report.⟦P15⟧This attribution will be added to the ⟦P16⟧, if one is published.⟦P17⟧Please keep reports succinct, including a description of the issue you've found,⟦P18⟧the way in which you believe it can be exploited, and a small reproduction test⟦P19⟧case or program which demonstrates the issue.⟦P20⟧We have a ⟦P21⟧list of decisions⟦PB1_22⟧ about commonly-reported⟦P23⟧categories of issues.⟦P24⟧Your email will be acknowledged within 7 days. If you have not received a reply⟦P25⟧by then, please follow up with the Go Security team again at⟦P26⟧⟦P27⟧⟦P28⟧⟦PB1_29⟧. Please make sure the word⟦P30⟧⟦P31⟧vulnerability⟦P32⟧ is in your email.⟦P33⟧If after 3 more days you have still not received an acknowledgement of your⟦P34⟧report, it is possible that your email might have been marked as spam. In that⟦P35⟧case, please ⟦P36⟧file an issue here⟦PB1_37⟧. Select ⟦P38⟧"I want to⟦P39⟧report a technical security or an abuse risk related bug in a Google product⟦P40⟧(SQLi, ⟦P41⟧, etc.)"⟦P42⟧, and list ⟦P43⟧"Go"⟦P44⟧ as the affected product.⟦P45⟧Your issue will be fixed or made public within 90 days after our initial⟦P46⟧acknowledgement.⟦P47⟧Emails reporting security issues will be retained indefinitely⟦P48⟧to track remediation status and correctly attribute discoveries.⟦P49⟧LLM-Generated Reports⟦P50⟧Please do not send LLM-generated reports without proper curation.⟦P51⟧Modern LLMs are very good at finding real and important security bugs.⟦P52⟧Unfortunately, they are also very good at finding imaginary issues, or real⟦P53⟧issues that are ⟦P54⟧not security bugs⟦PB1_55⟧. LLM⟦P56⟧output is often verbose, buries important information in irrelevant text, and⟦P57⟧contains grandiose and unsupportable claims.⟦P58⟧For LLM-generated reports, the value lies in curation. Sending us dozens of⟦P59⟧possible security issues while relying on us to find the one significant result⟦P60⟧hidden among them asks us to do the curation, and ultimately hinders our goal of⟦P61⟧making Go secure. For this reason, we only attribute discoveries to reporters⟦P62⟧with a substantial percentage of real findings.⟦P63⟧While there are no hard and fast rules, as a general guideline, if most of your⟦P64⟧reports are not designated as security issues, and we do not believe that you⟦P65⟧are providing useful curation, we will not credit you for any discoveries in the⟦P66⟧remainder. We deliberately do not define a concrete threshold for "most" or what⟦P67⟧"curation" entails. However, our intent is that a good-faith reporter providing⟦P68⟧meaningful value will always receive credit, while a reporter forwarding large⟦P69⟧quantities of LLM output indiscriminately will not.⟦P70⟧Tracks⟦P71⟧Depending on the nature of your issue, it will be categorized by the Go⟦P72⟧Security team as an issue in the PUBLIC, PRIVATE, or ⟦P73⟧ track. All security⟦P74⟧issues will be issued ⟦P75⟧ numbers.⟦P76⟧The Go Security team does not assign traditional fine-grained severity labels⟦P77⟧(e.g CRITICAL, HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW) to security issues because severity depends⟦P78⟧highly on how a user is using the affected ⟦P79⟧ or functionality. Additionally,⟦P80⟧when issuing CVEs for Go security issues we do not assign ⟦P81⟧ scores, as we⟦P82⟧fundamentally disagree with the applicability of the scoring system to Go for⟦P83⟧the same reasons. Third parties, such as MITRE or ⟦P84⟧, via the ⟦P85⟧, may assign⟦P86⟧⟦P87⟧ scores to our vulnerabilities, but we do not endorse these scores as⟦P88⟧accurate reflections of their impact.⟦P89⟧For example, the impact of a resource exhaustion issue in the ⟦P90⟧⟦P91⟧parser depends on what is being parsed. If the user is parsing trusted ⟦P92⟧⟦P93⟧files from their local filesystem, the impact is likely to be low. If the user⟦P94⟧is parsing untrusted arbitrary ⟦P95⟧ from an ⟦P96⟧ request body, the impact may be⟦P97⟧much higher.⟦P98⟧That said, the following issue tracks do signal how severe and/or wide-reaching⟦P99⟧the Security team believes an issue to be. For example, an issue with medium to⟦P100⟧significant impact for many users is a ⟦P101⟧ track issue in this policy, and⟦P102⟧an issue with negligible to minor impact, or which affects only a small subset⟦P103⟧of users, is a ⟦P104⟧ track issue.⟦P105⟧PUBLIC⟦P106⟧Issues in the ⟦P107⟧ track affect niche configurations, have very limited⟦P108⟧impact, or are already widely known.⟦P109⟧⟦P110⟧ track issues are labeled with⟦P111⟧⟦P112⟧⟦P113⟧⟦PB1_114⟧,⟦P115⟧discussed through the⟦P116⟧⟦P117⟧Go proposal review process⟦PB1_118⟧⟦P119⟧⟦P120⟧fixed in public⟦P121⟧, and get backported to the next scheduled ⟦P122⟧minor⟦P123⟧releases⟦PB1_124⟧ (which occur ~monthly). The release announcement⟦P125⟧includes details of these issues, but there is no pre-announcement.⟦P126⟧Examples of past PUBLIC issues include:⟦P127⟧⟦P128⟧#44916⟦PB1_129⟧: ⟦P130⟧: can panic when calling ⟦P131⟧⟦P132⟧⟦P133⟧#44913⟦PB1_134⟧: ⟦P135⟧: infinite loop when using ⟦P136⟧ with a custom ⟦P137⟧⟦P138⟧⟦P139⟧#43786⟦PB1_140⟧: ⟦P141⟧: incorrect operations on the ⟦P142⟧ curve⟦P143⟧⟦P144⟧#40928⟦PB1_145⟧: net/http/cgi,net/http/fcgi: Cross-Site Scripting (⟦P146⟧) when Content-Type is not specified⟦P147⟧⟦P148⟧#40618⟦PB1_149⟧: ⟦P150⟧: ⟦P151⟧ and ⟦P152⟧ can read an unlimited number of bytes from invalid inputs⟦P153⟧⟦P154⟧#36834⟦PB1_155⟧: ⟦P156⟧: certificate validation bypass on Windows 10⟦P157⟧PRIVATE⟦P158⟧Issues in the ⟦P159⟧ track are violations of committed security properties.⟦P160⟧⟦P161⟧ track issues are ⟦P162⟧fixed in the next scheduled ⟦P163⟧minor⟦P164⟧releases⟦PB1_165⟧⟦P166⟧, and are kept private until then.⟦P167⟧Three to seven days before the release, a pre-announcement is sent to⟦P168⟧golang-announce, announcing the presence of one or more security fixes in the⟦P169⟧upcoming releases, and whether the issues affect the standard library, the⟦P170⟧toolchain, or both, as well as reserved ⟦P171⟧ IDs for each of the fixes.⟦P172⟧For issues that are present in a ⟦P173⟧major version release candidate⟦PB1_174⟧,⟦P175⟧we follow the same process, including fixes in the next scheduled release⟦P176⟧candidate.⟦P177⟧Some examples of past PRIVATE issues include:⟦P178⟧⟦P179⟧#53416⟦PB1_180⟧: ⟦P181⟧: stack exhaustion in Glob⟦P182⟧⟦P183⟧#53616⟦PB1_184⟧: ⟦P185⟧: stack exhaustion in all Parse⟦P186⟧ functions⟦P187⟧⟦P188⟧#54658⟦PB1_189⟧: ⟦P190⟧: handle server errors after sending ⟦P191⟧⟦P192⟧⟦P193⟧#56284⟦PB1_194⟧: syscall, ⟦P195⟧: unsanitized ⟦P196⟧ in environment variables⟦P197⟧URGENT⟦P198⟧⟦P199⟧ track issues are a threat to the Go ecosystem’s integrity, or are being⟦P200⟧actively exploited in the wild leading to severe damage. There are no recent⟦P201⟧examples, but they would include remote code execution in ⟦P202⟧, or⟦P203⟧practical key recovery in ⟦P204⟧.⟦P205⟧⟦P206⟧ track issues are fixed in private, and ⟦P207⟧trigger an immediate dedicated⟦P208⟧security release⟦P209⟧, possibly with no pre-announcement.⟦P210⟧Flagging Existing Issues as Security-related⟦P211⟧If you believe that an ⟦P212⟧existing issue⟦PB1_213⟧ is security-related, we ask⟦P214⟧that you send an email to ⟦P215⟧⟦P216⟧⟦PB1_217⟧.⟦P218⟧The email should include the issue ⟦P219⟧ and a short description of why it should⟦P220⟧be handled according to this security policy.⟦P221⟧Disclosure Process⟦P222⟧The Go project uses the following disclosure process:⟦P223⟧Once the security report is received it is assigned a primary handler. This⟦P224⟧person coordinates the fix and release process.⟦P225⟧The issue is confirmed and a list of affected software is determined.⟦P226⟧Code is audited to find any potential similar problems.⟦P227⟧If it is determined, in consultation with the submitter, that a ⟦P228⟧ number⟦P229⟧is required, the primary handler will obtain one.⟦P230⟧Fixes are prepared for the two most recent major releases and the⟦P231⟧head/master revision. Fixes are prepared for the two most recent major releases⟦P232⟧and merged to head/master.⟦P233⟧On the date that the fixes are applied, announcements are sent to⟦P234⟧⟦P235⟧golang-announce⟦PB1_236⟧,⟦P237⟧⟦P238⟧golang-dev⟦PB1_239⟧, and⟦P240⟧⟦P241⟧golang-nuts⟦PB1_242⟧.⟦P243⟧This process can take some time, especially when coordination is required with⟦P244⟧maintainers of other projects. Every effort will be made to handle the bug in⟦P245⟧as timely a manner as possible, however it's important that we follow the⟦P246⟧process described above to ensure that disclosures are handled consistently.⟦P247⟧For security issues that include the assignment of a ⟦P248⟧ number, the issue is⟦P249⟧listed publicly under the⟦P250⟧⟦P251⟧"Golang" product on the CVEDetails website⟦PB1_252⟧⟦P253⟧as well as the⟦P254⟧⟦P255⟧National Vulnerability Disclosure site⟦PB1_256⟧.⟦P257⟧Receiving Security Updates⟦P258⟧The best way to receive security announcements is to subscribe to the⟦P259⟧⟦P260⟧golang-announce⟦PB1_261⟧⟦P262⟧mailing list. Any messages pertaining to a security issue will be prefixed with⟦P263⟧⟦P264⟧.⟦P265⟧Comments on This Policy⟦P266⟧If you have any suggestions to improve this policy, please⟦P267⟧⟦P268⟧file an issue⟦PB1_269⟧ for discussion.⟦P270⟧
